  "reading": "On 18,407 Mus musculus distal candidate cis-regulatory elements (ENCODE SCREEN mm10 registry v3) open in exactly one of 12 ENCODE embryonic-day-14.5 mouse tissues by the accessibility source's own replicated-peak calls (ENCODE ATAC-seq, embryonic day 14.5, 12 tissues) and lying in no protein-coding gene body, the gene with the nearest 5' end inside the element's domain, delimited by 4,796 domain edges of the 2,398 topological domains Bonev et al. 2017 call on mm10 in neural progenitors, embryonic neocortex, in vivo (Table S2, sheet `ncx NPC`), is expressed at 5 transcripts per million or more and highest in the element's own tissue, in RNA from that same tissue (ENCODE polyA plus RNA-seq, the same 12 tissues at the same stage), for 2,175 of 18,407 (11.8%), against 2,127 of 18,407 (11.6%) for the gene with the nearest 5' end on the chromosome; the rules differ on 2,352 of 18,407 elements; difference +0.26 points, 95% -0.01 to +0.53 over 1-Mb clusters. No difference detected between the boundary rule and nearest TSS. With 2,352 elements differing, a difference smaller than about 0.74 points could go undetected. Against the gene-structure rule on the same elements (2,171 of 18,407 (11.8%); the two differ on 4,027, and neither names a host here), the difference is +0.02 points, 95% -0.39 to +0.41. The boundary rule's genes match the element's tissue more often than genes at a matched distance (7.5%, one-sided p 0.001), so position carries tissue information here. With the same boundaries rotated along each chromosome the rule averages 11.5% on these elements (one-sided p 0.001), so the measured positions carry more than the boundaries' number and spacing. On all 44,021 distal elements (beside, deciding nothing; the rules differ on 4,553) the boundary rule is concordant for 5,298 of 44,021 (12.0%) against 5,295 of 44,021 (12.0%) for nearest TSS, +0.01 points [-0.16, +0.18], and against 5,338 of 44,021 (12.1%) for the gene-structure rule, -0.09 points [-0.36, +0.17]. The positive control separates: on 6,021 promoter-class elements the nearest-TSS gene is concordant for 11.3% against 7.6% at a matched distance (one-sided p 0.001). 0 of 55,221 attributed gene slots on this population name a gene absent from the RNA source's rows and are counted as not concordant. The mammal is asked here because the two invertebrates disagree about this very stratum: the worm's domain rule is ahead of nearest TSS between genes by +3.73 points [+2.79, +4.74] on 2,335 elements, surviving its outron exclusion at +4.47 [+3.16, +5.78] on 1,745, while the fly's is +0.12 [-0.25, +0.61] on 3,265 brain elements, no difference detected. Accessibility is not function, and a boundary is not a regulatory unit by itself: this says the attributed gene is expressed where the element is open, not that the element acts on it. The accessibility and the RNA come from different animals of the same strain and stage, not the same nuclei; a tissue is a mixture of cell types, so an element open in one tissue and a gene highest in that tissue can belong to different cells of it; the boundaries come from neural progenitors of embryonic neocortex and are applied to all twelve tissues, so they are stage-matched and tissue-matched to only three of them; and a domain's edges are read as its boundaries, which is the only conversion made to the boundary set.",
